Title: Hot-Foot Hannibal
Credit: Adapted from the tale by Charles W. Chesnutt (1899)
Order: 7
Production Code: 1x07

FADE IN:

EXT. THE WILLOW BRANCH - DUSK

AMB: a slow creek over pebbles, swamp frogs, bay-blossom scent you can almost hear

An amber-colored creek crossing a sandy road. A bay-tree in white blossom on this side; on the far side, a drooping willow, its leaves trailing in the water.

[[Uncle Julius McAdoo]] stands beside the road, hat in hand, looking across the stream at the willow. He turns to the lens.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
Fridays, now, a gray hoss kin see w'at w'ite folks can't. It's a gif'. Hit ain' no use ter argy wid it.

He looks back at the willow. There is, perhaps, a woman sitting under it. There is, perhaps, a trick of the light.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
She's been waitin' dere fo'ty yeahs. She's right patient. Mos'ly she's des lookin' fer a man.

MUSIC: STING - a single long violin note

JULIUS (CONT'D)
I doan ax you ter b'lieve it. I des ax you ter set a minute.

SUPER: "THE CONJURE WOMAN"

INT. THE VINEYARD - PARLOR - DAY

Venetian blinds half-closed against the heat. A lace curtain stirs. On the piazza outside, in a hammock behind a honeysuckle vine, [[John]] dozes with a hat over his face.

VOICES rise inside the parlor. [[Mabel]], at the piano, stands. [[Malcolm Murchison]], tall, proud, with his hat in his hand, faces her.

MABEL
I hate you and despise you! I wish never to see you or speak to you again!

MALCOLM
Very well. I will take care that henceforth you have no opportunity to do either.

He crosses the piazza in two strides without seeing John, and goes off down the lane at a furious pace. John lifts the hat from his face.

Inside, Mabel sits at the piano and begins to play, loudly, with a touch that is anger and pride and independence and a dash of exultation.

INT. THE VINEYARD - JOHN AND ANNIE'S ROOM - NIGHT

John, in his shirtsleeves, tells [[Annie]] what he overheard. She sits at her dressing-table brushing her hair.

ANNIE
I do not see how it can ever be settled. It is something more than a mere lovers' quarrel. It began, it is true, because she found fault with him for going to church with that hateful Branson girl. But before it ended there were things said that no woman of any spirit could stand.

JOHN
I hope it will be made up within a day or two.

ANNIE
I am afraid it is all over between them.

SUPER: "TEN DAYS LATER."

EXT. THE VINEYARD - FRONT PIAZZA - DAY

A pleasant afternoon. Julius drives the rockaway up to the steps. John, Annie, and Mabel, who looks neither right nor left, take their seats.

JOHN
Which way shall we go: the short road or the long one?

ANNIE
I guess we had better take the short road. We will get there sooner.

JULIUS
It's a mighty fine dribe roun' by de big road, Mis' Annie, en it doan take much longer ter git dere.

ANNIE
No. There is a bay-tree in blossom near the mineral spring, and I wish to get some of the flowers.

JULIUS
I 'spec's you 'd fin' some bay-trees 'long de big road, ma'am.

ANNIE
But I know about the flowers on the short road, and they are the ones I want.

Julius, with an unreadable face, lifts the reins.

EXT. THE SHORT ROAD - DAY

The road through the swamp: dark, umbrageous foliage on both sides, unbroken by any clearing. Cool. Quiet. John gathers a dozen white blossoms from a low bough. He resumes his seat.

The mare starts, goes a few rods, and stops short at the edge of a branch.

JOHN
Why did you stop, Julius?

JULIUS
I did n', suh. 'T wuz de mare stop'. G' 'long dere, Lucy! Wat you mean by dis foolis'ness?

He jerks the reins, applies the whip lightly. The mare does not stir.

JOHN
Perhaps you had better get down and lead her.

Julius alights, takes the bridle, and pulls. The mare plants her feet.

JOHN (CONT'D)
I have never known her to balk before. Have you?

JULIUS
No, suh. I neber has. It's a cu'ous thing ter me, suh.

JOHN
What's the best way to make her go?

JULIUS
I 'spec's, suh, dat ef I 'd tu'n her 'roun', she 'd go de udder way.

JOHN
But we want her to go this way.

JULIUS
Well, suh, I 'low ef we des set heah fo' er fibe minutes, she 'll sta't up by herse'f.

JOHN
All right. It is cooler here than any place I have struck today.

They sit in silence. The amber stream flows by. Julius, standing at the mare's head, goes suddenly still.

JULIUS
Uh huh! I knows w'y dis mare doan go. It des flash' 'cross my recommemb'ance.

JOHN
Why is it, Julius?

JULIUS
'Ca'se she sees Chloe.

JOHN
Where is Chloe?

JULIUS
Chloe's done be'n dead dese fo'ty years er mo'. Her ha'nt is settin' ober yander on de udder side er de branch, unner dat wilier-tree, dis blessed minute.

ANNIE
Why, Julius! Do you see the haunt?

JULIUS
No'm. I doan see 'er. But de mare sees 'er.

JOHN
How do you know?

JULIUS
Well, suh, dis yer is a gray hoss, en dis yer is a Friday; en a gray hoss kin alluz see a ha'nt w'at walks on Friday.

MABEL
(for the first time, softly)
Who was Chloe?

ANNIE
And why does Chloe's haunt walk?

JULIUS
It's all in de tale, ma'am. It's all in de tale.

JOHN
Tell us the tale. Perhaps by the time you get through, the haunt will go away and the mare will cross.

Julius takes off his hat, and leans on the rockaway wheel.

JULIUS
Chloe use' ter b'long ter ole Mars' Dugal' McAdoo,--my ole marster...

MUSIC: BED IN - slow strings, a distant, stately violin

SUPER: "BEFORE THE WAR."

INT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - PARLOR - DAY (BEFORE THE WAR)

A white-columned room. A young woman in a maid's apron, graceful, quick, with a self-possessed tilt to her head: [[Chloe]]. At her side, a gentlewoman in a high collar, [[Old Miss]], directing her in the folding of linen.

JULIUS (V.O.)
She wuz a lackly gal en a smart gal, en ole mis' tuk her up ter de big house, en l'arnt her ter wait on de w'ite folks, 'tel bimeby she come ter be mis's own maid.

Two young men in the doorway, hats in hand: [[Hannibal]], sharp and supple, and [[Jeff]], lighter-skinned, open-faced. [[Mars Dugal' McAdoo]] looks them over.

MARS DUGAL'
We lacks Hannibal de bes', en we gwine ter keep him. Heah, Hannibal, you 'll wuk at de house fum now on. En ef you er a good nigger en min's yo' bizness, I 'll gib you Chloe fer a wife nex' spring. You udder nigger, you Jeff, you kin go back ter de qua'ters.

Chloe, behind Old Miss, has been listening. Her eyes go to Jeff; she makes up her mind about both men on the spot.

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - PATH TO THE QUARTERS - DAY

Chloe slips round the house and intercepts Jeff.

CHLOE
Doan be down-hea'ted. I's gwine ter see ef I kin fin' some way er 'nuther ter git rid er dat nigger Hannibal, en git you up ter de house in his place.

INT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - HALL - DAY

Hannibal, in a house-boy's white coat, quick as lightning, a cocksure grin, carries a tray and swells past Chloe.

HANNIBAL
Come heah en kiss me, honey. You gwine ter be mine in de spring. You doan 'pear ter be ez fon' er me ez you oughter be.

CHLOE
(coolly)
I ain' got no time fer you.

She walks off. He grins after her, quite undisturbed.

EXT. THE QUARTERS - EVENING

Chloe presses a silver dollar and a silk handkerchief into Jeff's hand.

CHLOE
Go down ter Aun' Peggy, de cunjuh 'oman down by de Wim'l'ton Road. Ax her ter gib you sump'n ter he'p git Hannibal out'n de big house, so de w'ite folks 'll sen' fer you ag'in.

EXT. THE FREE SETTLEMENT - NIGHT

Jeff, at the door of a dark cabin, hat in hand. [[Aun' Peggy]] takes the dollar and the handkerchief and says nothing. The door closes.

EXT. THE FREE SETTLEMENT - NIGHT (NEXT NIGHT)

She puts in his hands a doll: a body of corn-stalk, splinter arms and legs, a head of elderberry pith, two little red peppers for feet.

AUN' PEGGY
Dis yer baby doll is Hannibal. Dis yer peth head is Hannibal's head, en dese yer pepper feet is Hannibal's feet. Hide it unner de house, on de sill unner de do', whar Hannibal 'll hafter walk ober it eve'y day. En ez long ez Hannibal comes anywhar nigh dis baby doll, he 'll be des lack it is,--light-headed en hot-footed. En w'en you git all th'oo wid it, you mus' fetch it back ter me, fer it's monst'us powerful goopher, en is liable ter make mo' trouble ef you leabe it layin' roun'.

EXT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - NIGHT

Chloe leads Jeff to the crawl-space under the house. He lays the doll on the sill under the door and creeps back out. They stand together in the dark; she smiles.

SFX: a hornet's drone, brief, and gone

INT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - HALL - MORNING

Hannibal, with an armload of wood, crosses the sill. He stops. His face changes. He drops the wood.

HANNIBAL
(hopping)
Oh! Oh!

Upstairs, a door opens. Old Miss, in her wrapper, an hour too early, looks down the stairs with ice in her face.

OLD MISS
Hannibal.

HANNIBAL
(dancing on his heels)
Yas'm. Mist'iss.

INT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - DINING-ROOM - DAY

A big dish of chicken and dumplings, carried by Hannibal and the cook. At the door the sill is under his foot. His feet burn. His head swims. The dish falls in the dust of the yard. The white folks dine on cold ham and sweet potatoes.

INT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - HALL - DAY

Mars Dugal' sends Hannibal on an errand.

MARS DUGAL'
Go ober ter Mars' Marrabo Utley's en borry a monkey wrench.

Hannibal returns hours later with a screw-driver. Mars Dugal' sends another man to take it back; Hannibal gets no dinner.

EXT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - FLOWER GARDEN - AFTERNOON

Hannibal, weeding, digs up every flower bulb in Old Miss's front garden, the ones sent for and paid for dear, and carries them in his apron to the hog-pen. The hogs eat them.

SFX: hogs, contented

Old Miss, in the cool of the evening, sees. She is almost speechless. She writes a note and hands it to Hannibal, to carry to the overseer.

EXT. THE QUARTERS - DAY

Hannibal, in the corn-field with the field-hands, hoe in hand, the overseer, Mr. Smif, cursing and roaring above him. They call him names; one of them says "Hot-Foot".

JULIUS (V.O.)
Mars' Dugal' tuk Hannibal back down ter de qua'ters. De w'ite folks could n' stan' it no longer.

INT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - HALL - DAY

Jeff, in the white coat, bowing to Old Miss, who looks him over and likes him. Chloe at the foot of the stairs, hands folded, no expression at all.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Chloe en Jeff wuz so int'rusted in one ernudder sence Jeff be'n up ter de house, dat dey fergot all 'bout takin' de baby doll back ter Aun' Peggy.

On the sill, under the door, in the dust, the little doll lies unnoticed with its red-pepper feet.

EXT. THE CORN-FIELD - DAY

Hannibal, in a row of corn, sweating, listening to the hands behind him laugh. His face hardens.

JULIUS (V.O.)
De mo' Hannibal studied 'bout it de mo' madder he got, 'tel he fin'lly swo' he wuz gwine ter git eben wid Jeff en Chloe, ef it wuz de las' ac'.

EXT. THE ROAD - SUNDAY - DAY

Hannibal, hidden in the corn at the edge of the road. Chloe passes. He steps out in front of her.

HANNIBAL
Hoddy, Chloe?

CHLOE
I ain' got no time fer ter fool wid fiel'-han's. W'at you want wid me, Hot-Foot?

HANNIBAL
I wants ter know how you en Jeff is gittin' 'long.

CHLOE
Me en Jeff is gittin' 'long mighty well, en we gwine ter git married in de spring, en you ain' gwine ter be 'vited ter de weddin' nuther.

HANNIBAL
(with enormous politeness)
No, no, I would n' 'spec' ter be 'vited ter de weddin'. I's glad ter heah you en Jeff is gittin' 'long so well. I did n' knowed but w'at he had 'mence' ter be a little ti'ed.

CHLOE
Ti'ed er me? Dat's rediklus. Dat nigger lubs me so I b'liebe he 'd go th'oo fire en water fer me.

HANNIBAL
Uh huh. Den I reckon it mus' be some udder nigger w'at meets a 'oman down by de crick in de swamp eve'y Sunday ebenin'.

CHLOE
You is a liah.

HANNIBAL
Mebbe I is a liah, en mebbe I ain' got good eyes. But 'less'n I is, Jeff is gwine ter meet dat 'oman dis ebenin' 'long 'bout eight o'clock right down dere by de crick in de swamp.

She calls him a low-down nigger, a slanderer. She walks away fast. She does not run.

INT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - PARLOR - DUSK

Chloe by a window, working it out. A girl she knew at the Utley place; two or three evenings in the last week when she had been at the ladies' dressing and Jeff had been free. Little things she had not noticed.

EXT. THE SHORT ROAD - EVENING

Chloe, running down the road, keeping to the bushes. She stops at the edge of the branch: it is the same branch, the same amber water, the same willow drooping over the creek on the far side.

On the far bank, under the willow, Jeff sits and looks up the road toward the Utley plantation.

A woman in a sunbonnet comes down the road. Jeff jumps up, runs, throws both arms round her neck.

Chloe does not wait to see more. She turns and runs.

INT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - PIAZZA - NIGHT

Chloe, panting, in front of Mars Dugal' and Old Miss on the piazza. She tells them all about the baby doll, about Jeff, about Aun' Peggy.

INT. UNDER THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - NIGHT

Mars Dugal', on his knees with a lantern, finds the doll. He holds it up, and turns white as chalk.

MARS DUGAL'
Wat debil's wuk is dis? No wonder de po' nigger's feet eetched. Sump'n got ter be done ter l'arn dat ole witch ter keep her han's off'n my niggers. En ez fer dis yer Jeff, I'm gwine ter do des w'at I promus.

Old Miss, behind him, says nothing. She has not forgotten the bulbs.

EXT. PATESVILLE - STEAMBOAT WHARF - MORNING

A steamboat, smoke at the funnel. Jeff, on the gangplank beside a trader. He looks back at the wharf, once, as if looking for someone. The whistle blows.

SFX: a steam whistle, long and low

MUSIC: STING - a long violin note

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - ROAD - DAY

Chloe, with her head high, walking down the road as if she were glad. In front of her, Hannibal, doubled up with laughter.

HANNIBAL
Yah, yah, yah! Oh, hol' me, honey, hol' me, er I'll laf myse'f ter def. I ain' nebber laf' so much sence I be'n bawn.

CHLOE
Wat you laffin' at, Hot-Foot?

HANNIBAL
W'y, I's laffin' at myse'f, tooby sho',--laffin' ter think w'at a fine 'oman I made.

Chloe goes pale. She puts out a hand to a bush by the road.

CHLOE
Wat you mean by de kin' er 'oman you made?

HANNIBAL
I means dat I got squared up wid you fer treatin' me de way you done, en I got eben wid dat yaller nigger Jeff fer cuttin' me out. I sont wo'd ter Jeff dat Sunday dat you wuz gwine ter be ober ter Mars' Marrabo's visitin' dat ebenin', en you want 'im ter meet you down by de crick on de way home. En den I put on a frock en a sunbonnet, en fix' myse'f up ter look lack a 'oman; en w'en Jeff seed me comin', he run ter meet me, en you seed 'im. En now I reckon you en Jeff bofe knows w'at it means ter mess wid a nigger lack me.

Her knees give way. She falls in the road.

MUSIC: BED IN - slow violins, minor

INT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - CHLOE'S ROOM - NIGHT

Chloe, thin, at a window, looking toward the swamp. A doctor leans over her; in the corridor Old Miss and Mars Dugal' speak low.

MARS DUGAL'
She kin hab de noo house boy fer a husban'.

OLD MISS
Chloe ain' dat kin'er gal. You sh'd buy Jeff back.

INT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - STUDY - DAY

Mars Dugal' writing. The letter, on the desk, a few lines. A reply, weeks later, unsealed in his hand:

MARS DUGAL'
(reading aloud, flat)
"Jeff fell ove'boa'd er jumped off'n de steamboat on de way ter Wim'l'ton, en got drownded, en co'se I could n' sell 'im back, much ez I 'd lack ter 'bleedge you."

He puts it down. Old Miss, at his shoulder, takes it up and reads it.

EXT. THE SHORT ROAD - EVENING

Chloe, walking down the road alone as if to meet someone. She sits under the willow on the far side of the branch. She waits. Night after night.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Chloe got ter slippin' down here in de ebenin' des lack she 'uz comin' ter meet Jeff, en she 'd set dere unner dat wilier-tree on de udder side, en wait fer 'im, night atter night.

EXT. THE SHORT ROAD - DAWN

The branch. Morning mist. A shape in the amber water, face down, under the willow. Two men standing on the bank, hats off.

MUSIC: STING - one long violin note, held

EXT. THE WILLOW BRANCH - DUSK (PRESENT)

Julius, finishing, with the willow behind him. He looks across the creek. The light has gone coppery.

JULIUS
Eber sence den, Chloe's ha'nt comes eve'y ebenin' en sets down unner dat willer-tree en waits fer Jeff, er e'se walks up en down de road yander, lookin' en lookin', en waitin' en waitin', fer her sweethea't w'at ain' neber, neber come back ter her no mo'.

Silence. Annie wipes her eyes. Mabel, pale, has a tear on her cheek. John is looking at the road.

ANNIE
I think, Julius, that you may turn the mare around and go by the long road.

Julius obeys with alacrity. The mare, turned, steps away at an easy trot.

JOHN
(jocular)
You are not afraid of Chloe's haunt, are you?

Neither of the ladies smiles.

ANNIE
Oh, no. I've changed my mind. I prefer the other route.

EXT. THE BIG ROAD - DAY

A cart driven by a young man, with a trunk and a valise in the back. Marshall, Malcolm's servant, tips his hat.

JOHN
Who's going away, Marshall?

MARSHALL
Young Mistah Ma'colm gwine 'way on de boat ter Noo Yo'k dis ebenin', suh, en I's takin' his things down ter de wharf, suh. He's comin' 'long behin', suh, en I 'spec's you'll meet 'im up de road a piece. He 'spec's ter be gone a long time, suh, en say prob'ly he ain' neber comin' back.

The cart drives on. Mabel, rigid, looks at her hands. Annie speaks to her in an undertone that John does not catch.

ANNIE
Julius, you may stop the rockaway a moment. There are some trumpet-flowers by the road there that I want. Will you get them for me, John?

John climbs down, goes into the underbrush, and returns with an armful of scarlet blossoms. The carriage seat is empty beside Annie.

JOHN
Where is Mabel?

ANNIE
She has walked on ahead. We shall overtake her in a few minutes.

They drive a little way.

ANNIE (CONT'D)
(feeling in her lap)
I had it where we were stopping. I have dropped my fan. Julius, will you go back and get it for me?

Julius, with perfect gravity, climbs down and goes back. He is an unconscionably long time finding it.

EXT. THE BIG ROAD - LATER

The carriage on the road. Ahead, two figures walking arm in arm, with a long shadow on the road: Mabel and Malcolm, their faces aglow with the light of love.

MUSIC: BED IN - strings, major, wide open

JOHN
(slowly)
Well, I'll be--

He looks at his wife. She is looking straight ahead. Julius, on the box, is studying the mare's ears.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - DUSK

Julius on the log, with the vines behind him. His coachman's coat is the same; the log is the same. On his knee, his hat.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
I doan know whuther I had a 'standin' wid young Mistah Ma'colm. Dat's fer Mistah John ter 'spicion. But I knows he's gwine ter set up housekeepin' ober at de ole Murchison place, en I knows he'd a-lacked ter hab me.

He puts the hat on.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
I done said no. Somebody got ter look atter de grapes.

He looks out at the vineyard. The mare, in the stable, is silent.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Dat's all I got fer you dis season. Dey's mo' tales. Dey's alluz mo' tales. De groun' ain' done talkin'.

MUSIC: BED IN - the series theme in full

JULIUS (CONT'D)
I doan ax you ter b'lieve it. I des ax you ter set a minute.

He lifts a hand, as if to a friend across a road. Wind in the vines.

FADE OUT.

THE END
